{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>Akh.</b> Now yukam, which is of a binyan whose verbal noun is not called [by a distinct form], is like yinakem, which is of the nif'al binyan. The Samaritans said that the meaning of \"nakom yinakem\" is a great vengeance, to punish him, and if he stands one day, the vengeance is lesser, and if two days, he is not avenged at all. Their proof is that we do not find the word \"nakamah\" in [cases of] court-imposed death penalties, and they said that \"a revenging sword\" is not a complete proof that it is killing, and furthermore, after Scripture said \"a day,\" what need is there to say \"two days\"? And all this was difficult for them, that the master should be killed on account of the death of the slave. Now I shall answer them: why did Scripture not specify what the great or small vengeance is? Let us pursue the verses: \"avenge the vengeance of the children of Israel\" (Numbers 31:2), and all the Midianites were killed. And Samson said, \"If I am avenged of you\" (Judges 15:7), and after it is written, \"and he struck them leg upon thigh\" (ibid. v. 8). \"Let me avenge one vengeance\" (ibid. 16:28), and they died in the house, and a two-edged sword in their hand (Psalms 149:6), and after it, \"to execute vengeance upon the nations\" (ibid. v. 7). \"For the blood of His servants He will avenge\" (Deuteronomy 32:43) — and blood indicates killing. And in this manner all the language and the verse that stated this death with the word \"vengeance,\" for the master is punished through his slave on account of his being in his domain, for he cannot strike a fellow Israelite, his brother. And this is what is said, \"a day or two days\" — and it is like it: \"by the mouth of two witnesses\" (ibid. 17:6). And they said they did not know its reason; if so, let them place this alongside it. And our Sages, of blessed memory, said that the reason for \"a day or two days\" is that the hours [of a day and two days] are twenty-four hours in a day. And when we examined it, we found that it speaks of a staff, which is a staff of discipline, as in \"you shall strike him with a staff\" (Proverbs 23:14). Now the master intends to discipline him, and Scripture did not mention, when the master kills his slave, what the judgment upon him should be, for it is the master's way to have pity on his money, not to kill him, just as he has pity even on his animal. And HaShem commanded that when the master disciplines his slave, he must not discipline him with cruel discipline, for when he sees that his soul is departing under the staff, he must leave off; and if he does not leave off, he shall be killed on his account, for HaShem's mercy is upon all His works. And it is written of David: \"and blood in abundance you have shed\" (I Chronicles 22:8) — and they were not of Israel."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:37:09.695280558Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}